/* A WIDER GUTTER.
   22px + 21px put 43px between the two text columns, and the
   dashed rule sits in the middle of it — so each column cleared
   the rule by about 20px. That was enough while every block was
   text, and stopped being enough once matrices arrived: a grid
   reaches its full width with no rag, so the two columns met the
   rule squarely and read as one block of content with a line
   drawn through it. 32px a side gives 64px, and costs each
   column 10px of measure. */
.acol:first-child { padding-right:32px; }
.acols::after { content:""; position:absolute; top:0; height:1432px;
          left:calc((100% - 2.5px) / 2); width:0;
          border-left:2.5px dashed #5b8dd6; pointer-events:none; }

/* TABLE BODY IS READING TEXT.
   14.5px prints at 10.6px — under the 11px floor, and a table in a
   column is the densest reading on the page: rows a student scans
   for a value. 15.2px prints at 11.2px. This accounted for 164 of
   the 200 findings the corrected legibility gate surfaced. */
.acol .tbl { font-size:15.2px; }
